{"id":"salmon_wild_vs_farmed","buying_tips":["Wild salmon eats krill and small fish; farmed eats plant protein and vegetable oil.","Frozen wild Alaskan is the same fish, frozen at its best. ‘Fresh’ at the counter is often previously-frozen, so ask.","Sockeye and coho are almost always wild, and their color is deep because of diet, not dye.","A 2004 Science study found 14 contaminants, PCBs and dioxins included, all higher in farmed salmon. Levels were well below FDA tolerance levels for bought fish.","Canned wild salmon comes next; for farmed salmon, read the country of origin."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"shrimp_imported_vs_domestic","buying_tips":["Country of origin is printed by law, and wild American traces to a managed fishery.","‘Fresh’ at the case is nearly always thawed, so frozen raw is the same shrimp with more control over cooking.","A 2015 Consumer Reports test found antibiotics in 11 of 205 raw imported pond-raised samples, from Vietnam, Thailand and Bangladesh.","Second choice is pond-raised shrimp labeled Naturland or Aquaculture Stewardship Council; last is imported shrimp.","Ask the case if the shrimp is dry. Wet shrimp holds extra water."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"label_nonGMO_vs_organic","buying_tips":["Organic also restricts synthetic pesticides and fertilizers; non-GMO covers one issue.","Ignore ‘non-GMO’ on foods that have no GMO version anyway.","Look near the ingredients for “bioengineered” or “Contains Bioengineered Food Ingredients”, rather than “GMO”. A phone number or QR code may lead to the disclosure, which does not name the ingredient.","Bioengineered means a new gene was inserted into a plant or animal. The required statement applies only when modified DNA remains.","Choose certified organic first, with Non-GMO Project Verified a close second. Next is an unlabeled package; read any bioengineered disclosure."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"canned_fish_choosing","buying_tips":["‘Vegetable oil’ on a tin is nearly always a seed oil blend it will not name.","Bone-in and skin-on sardines and salmon. The bones soften completely.","The list should read fish, oil or water, salt. Anything longer is a sauce.","Skipjack, labeled ‘light’, is lower-mercury than albacore, labeled ‘white’.","Pick a can that says BPA-free; many food cans have an epoxy lining made with BPA. Fresh or frozen fish has no can lining."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"label_wild_vs_farm_raised","buying_tips":["A bare fillet is far easier to swap for another species than whole or skin-on fish.","Origin can mean where it was processed, not where it was caught; a staffed counter can usually say.","Cooked and breaded seafood falls outside the labeling rule.","‘Color added’ on farm-raised salmon means astaxanthin in the feed sets the pink, not a dye after harvest. Wild salmon get their color from krill and shrimp.","For salmon, choose wild-caught first. For farm-raised salmon, read the country of origin."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"tortillas","buying_tips":["Masa is corn cooked and soaked in limewater; some packaged corn tortillas add wheat.","For a flour tortilla, check the flour and the named fat rather than infer them from the color.","Most U.S. corn is bioengineered. Corn becomes grain, syrup, oil and starch.","Choose certified organic corn tortillas first, with Non-GMO Project Verified a close second."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"oat_milk","buying_tips":["Oat drinks vary carton to carton in stabilizers, protein and added vitamins.","Read the ingredient list for stabilizers, and the carton for protein and added vitamins and minerals.","Some certified organic oat drinks still use carrageenan, a seaweed thickener; pick one without it."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"canned_tuna","buying_tips":["FDA puts canned light tuna in Best Choices for mercury, and albacore in Good Choices.","Read the packing medium separately from the light or white designation.","Pick a can that says BPA-free; many food cans use an epoxy lining made with BPA. Fresh or frozen tuna comes with no can lining."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"smoked_salmon","buying_tips":["Shelf-stable smoked fish is heat-treated and sealed; nova and lox rely on the cold.","Pick it from a refrigerated case and put it in the fridge soon after buying.","Choose canned or jarred smoked fish to keep on the shelf.","Wild salmon get their color from krill and shrimp. Farmed salmon flesh is gray until astaxanthin in the feed sets the pink, which ‘color added’ means.","Choose smoked wild salmon first. For farmed smoked salmon, read the country of origin."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"almond_milk","buying_tips":["Almond drinks vary in added sugars, so the carton panel is the guide.","Pick unsweetened over sweetened, vanilla or chocolate.","Compare protein, vitamin D, calcium and potassium across cartons.","Check saturated fat on the panel.","Some certified organic almond drinks still use carrageenan, a seaweed thickener; choose a carton with none listed."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"soy_milk","buying_tips":["Every addition is another ingredient; soybeans and water stays closest to the bean.","A short-list unsweetened almond milk ranks above soy milk.","Dairy shoppers can read the Milk card.","Carrageenan, a seaweed thickener, appears in some certified organic foods too; choose a soy milk with none listed.","Most U.S. soybeans are bioengineered, so choose certified organic first, with Non-GMO Project Verified a close second."],"watch_out":["FDA counts only fortified soy in the dairy group, yet fortifying adds vitamins and minerals, each another ingredient.","Soy lecithin and soy protein come from the same soybeans. A QR code or phone number may lead to the bioengineered disclosure."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"sugars","buying_tips":["Cane and beet sugar are the same substance, crystallized from the plant’s juice.","Pick light or dark brown sugar; dark carries more molasses.","Keep brown sugar closed, since it hardens in open air.","More than half of granulated sugar sold comes from bioengineered sugar beets. Plain “sugar” names no crop.","Choose certified organic sugar first; organic must come from non-GMO plants. Non-GMO Project Verified is a close second."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"cooking_oils","buying_tips":["“Vegetable oil” on a label names a category, without any seed or fruit.","Read the label for the oil’s name first, then refined or unrefined.","Skip sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn and cottonseed oils; the last four come from mostly bioengineered crops.","Skip avocado oil too: the label cannot prove the oil is pure.","Read the list for BHA, BHT, TBHQ or propyl gallate, preservatives added to oils, and choose a list without them."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"canned_coconut_milk","buying_tips":["Many cans add a gum such as guar gum to thicken the milk and hold it steady.","Read the ingredients for guar gum or another gum.","Compare two cans by how much the list holds beyond coconut.","Pick a can that says BPA-free; many cans have an epoxy lining made with BPA. Where the same coconut milk is sold in glass, the jar has no can lining."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"canned_soup","buying_tips":["Low sodium is a fixed ceiling; reduced sodium only means less than a named soup.","Leave cans with deep dents, seam dents, leaks, rust or bulging lids on the shelf.","Store cans cool and dry, between 50 °F and 70 °F, with the oldest in front.","Wipe the lid before opening, and move opened soup to a covered container in the refrigerator.","Pick a can that says BPA-free; many food cans are lined with an epoxy made with BPA. Fresh or frozen soup has no can lining."],"watch_out":["“No salt added” is not “sodium free”; the soup can still carry sodium.","BPA-free is the maker’s word. EWG’s survey found makers define it as they wish. Makers that dropped BPA did not disclose the lining that replaced it."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"tortilla_chips","buying_tips":["Lime-cooked corn grinds into masa, a dough; untreated cornmeal cannot form one.","Check the frying fat in the ingredients; choose masa chips cooked in olive oil, coconut oil, tallow or lard.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Most U.S. corn is bioengineered. Corn becomes grain, syrup, oil and starch.","Choose certified organic tortilla chips first, with Non-GMO Project Verified a close second."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}
{"id":"popcorn","buying_tips":["Plain kernels pop with no oil, so any butter, oil or salt is your call.","Microwave and ready-popped bags name their oil in the list; skip sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn and cottonseed oil.","Pop kernels at home in coconut oil, butter or tallow, then salt to taste.","Loose kernels pop in a plain brown paper bag in the microwave.","CSPI says popcorn is never bioengineered, so a non-GMO seal on plain kernels adds nothing."],"pinnedTipsMatch":true}

